 ## Overview
-This method generates and iteratively updates a 3D Signed Distance Field (SDF) volume on the GPU using a ping-pong buffering scheme. It solves the problem of maintaining a valid distance field after local geometric modifications (such as mouse-driven carving or addition) by combining immediate ray-casting-based brush application with iterative Eikonal equation propagation [S1, unofficial]. It differs from static SDF generation by supporting real-time user input and from simple voxel carving by ensuring the resulting field remains a valid distance function through distance propagation [S1, unofficial].
+This method generates and interactively mutates a 3D Signed Distance Field (SDF) on the GPU using a ping-pong buffering scheme to maintain data consistency during concurrent read/write operations. It solves the problem of updating a volumetric scalar field in real-time while preserving the Eikonal equation constraint ($|\nabla d(\mathbf{x})| = 1$) after local geometric modifications. It differs from static SDF generation by supporting real-time user input via volumetric brushes and from simple voxel carving by ensuring the resulting field remains a valid distance function through iterative Eikonal propagation [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
 **Inputs and Outputs:**
-*   **Input:** A 3D SDF volume stored in a ping-pong buffer, mouse position/direction, brush parameters (radius, height), and a boolean flag for Eikonal propagation.
-*   **Output:** An updated 3D SDF volume written to the alternate buffer.
+*   **Input:** A 3D SDF volume stored in a ping-pong buffer of two `RFloat` 3D render textures. Mouse position, direction, and brush parameters.
+*   **Output:** The updated SDF volume written to the active output texture.
 
